# Example remote state backend for organizers who want shared Terraform
# state across multiple facilitators/co-organizers instead of the default
# local state file.
#
# This file is INACTIVE by design (its extension is .tf.example, not .tf) so
# that a fresh participant clone always defaults to local state -- which is
# the correct choice for a single participant working inside one resource
# group with only Owner access there.
#
# To use it:
#   1. Have your subscription administrator provision (or reuse) a storage
#      account + container dedicated to Terraform state, OUTSIDE the
#      participant's workshop resource group (organizer-owned, not
#      participant-owned).
#   2. Copy this file to infra/backend.tf and fill in the values below.
#   3. Run `terraform -chdir=infra init -reconfigure` to migrate state.
#
# Terraform state can contain resource IDs and other data that should be
# treated as sensitive; never commit backend.tf, *.tfstate, or the
# organizer's storage account key to source control. Prefer
# `use_azuread_auth = true` (Entra ID) over an access key when the
# organizer's storage account has RBAC data-plane roles assigned.

# terraform {
#   backend "azurerm" {
#     resource_group_name  = "<organizer-owned-state-rg>"
#     storage_account_name = "<organizer-owned-state-storage-account>"
#     container_name       = "tfstate"
#     key                  = "foundry-handson/<participant-or-cohort-id>.tfstate"
#     use_azuread_auth     = true
#   }
# }
